# 8. ENVIRONMENT FILE RULES

`.env` files containing real secrets must never be committed.

Generate/update:

```text
.env.example
```

with placeholders only.

Example:

```text
SUPABASE_URL=<SET_IN_SECURE_ENVIRONMENT>
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=<SET_IN_SECURE_ENVIRONMENT>
PAYMENT_API_KEY=<SET_IN_SECURE_ENVIRONMENT>
```

Never put real secrets into:

```text
.env.example
README
workflow YAML
source code
logs
reports
HTML reports
GitHub comments
build artifacts
```

---

# 9. PUBLIC VS SERVER-SIDE VARIABLES

Classify every environment variable.

For frontend-exposed variables, verify that they are intentionally public.

For example, framework-specific public prefixes must be treated as public by design.

Never assume that a variable is secret merely because it is named `KEY`.

Never expose:

```text
service-role keys
private signing keys
database passwords
server credentials
payment secrets
OAuth client secrets
```

to browser code.

If a sensitive credential is reachable from the client bundle:

```text
BLOCK
```

---

# 10. REQUIRED ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE AUDIT

Build an inventory:

| Variable | Required | Environment | Public/Secret | Location | Status |
|---|---:|---|---|---|---|
| ... | ... | Production | ... | Vercel/GitHub/Supabase | ... |

Verify every required variable is available in the correct target.

Do not assume that a variable configured in GitHub automatically exists in Vercel.

Do not assume that a Vercel variable automatically exists inside Supabase Edge Functions.

---

# 11. VERCEL VARIABLE VERIFICATION

The generated workflow must verify that required Vercel environment variables exist for the correct environment.

Check:

```text
Production
Preview
Development
```

only where the project requires them.

Never print secret values.

If a required production variable is missing:

```text
BLOCK
```

If the workflow cannot verify the presence of a required variable because the necessary permissions are absent:

```text
BLOCKED — CANNOT VERIFY
```

Do not interpret inability to verify as success.

